  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDeep troughing across the eastern seaboard continues to slowly swing\neast toward the Atlantic, wrapping vort lobes around its base. Any\nresidual showers from these impulses of energy will taper off this\nevening. Lingering cold air advection and thinning clouds will allow\ntemperatures to fall into the upper 20s tonight in east central\nIN/west central OH. Some cloud cover remains in central OH/ northern\nKY, keeping temps a tad warmer. \n\nA subtle warming trend begins on Saturday, as ridging and surface\nhigh pressure begins to build in. However, temperatures still remain\nabout 5 to 10 degrees below normal Saturday and Sunday as the\nresidual cold airmass is slow to be shunted east out of the Great\nLakes region. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nMid level ridging moves in, ushering in height rises across the Ohio\nValley. Closer to the surface, a 1030+ high moves into the Great\nLakes area, continuing the dry and quiet conditions. By Monday,\ntemperatures reach near normals in the low 50s and temperatures\nshould return to above normal by Wednesday. \n\nSoutherly flow returns to the region on Wednesday, increasing the\nwarm air advection and warming trend. Long range ensemble guidance\nbegins to suggest that things turn more active as we head toward \nlate week, with a longwave trough deepening over western CONUS.",
